    "# 04. Calibration\n",
    "\n",
<<<<<<< HEAD
    "A model is **calibrated** if, among the loans it gives a 2% chance of default, about 2% really default. Notebook 03 found that the models are right on average in the calm years but far too low in 2006 and 2007. Here I look at that properly:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **How wrong, and in what way?** Is the model only too low overall (a shift in level), or are its probabilities also too spread out or too narrow (a shift in slope)?\n",
    "2. **Does recalibration fix it?** Platt scaling and isotonic regression, fitted on the training years only, as a bank would do in practice.\n",
    "3. **How much could a correction fix?** If the new default rate were known, how much of the gap would a simple correction close? The \"oracle\" versions use the test year's own labels, which a bank does not have when it scores the loans, so they show an upper limit and are not a method.\n",
    "\n",
    "Models and predictions come from notebook 03. The recalibration is fitted on the **out-of-fold predictions of the training years** (predictions for loans the model did not see during fitting). Nothing is fitted on 2005, 2006 or 2007, except the clearly marked oracle versions."
