    "## 1. Measures of calibration\n",
    "\n",
<<<<<<< HEAD
    "- **Predicted / actual**: the average predicted rate divided by the real rate. 1 is perfect, 0.5 means the model predicts half of what happens.\n",
    "- **Level shift**: how far the model's probabilities would have to be pushed on the log-odds scale to match the real rate. Positive means the model is too low. For example 1.5 means the odds are about 4.5 times higher than the model says.\n",
    "- **Calibration slope**: fit y on the model's log-odds. A slope of 1 is ideal. Below 1 means the predictions are too extreme (too spread out), above 1 means they are not spread out enough.\n",
    "- **Reliability and resolution** (Brier score split): reliability is the part of the error due to miscalibration (lower is better), resolution is how well the model separates loans (higher is better).\n",
    "- **ECE**: the average gap between predicted and actual rate over ten equal-size groups of loans.\n",
    "- **Binomial test**: could the number of defaults be a chance deviation from what the model expects? With rare defaults the groups are small, so I read ECE with care."
