   "source": "## 5. What I see\n\n**How wrong, and in what way?**\n- In-time the models are calibrated. The predicted / actual ratio is 0.98 to 1.00, the calibration slope is 0.94 to 0.98 (the 95% intervals contain 1), and the binomial test finds no sign of miscalibration (p = 0.53 to 0.99).\n- Out of time they are too low, and more so each year. Predicted / actual for the logistic model is 0.74 (2005), 0.42 (2006) and 0.18 (2007), and for boosting 0.69, 0.47 and 0.21. The binomial test rejects calibration in every one of those years (p below 0.0001).\n- On the log-odds scale the models would need pushing up by 0.33, 0.93 and 1.89 (logistic). That means the odds of default are about 1.4, 2.5 and 6.6 times higher than the model says.\n- It is not only a shift in level. The calibration slope is 0.78, 0.78 and 0.71 (logistic) and 0.89, 0.69 and 0.63 (boosting), below 1, so the predictions are also too spread out. The reliability diagrams show this: in 2007 the lowest-risk tenth of loans is predicted at 0.04% and actually defaults at 0.44% (about 12 times more), while the highest-risk tenth is predicted at 4.7% and defaults at 16.4% (about 3.5 times more). For boosting the factors are 10 and 2.5.\n- The ordering of loans holds. In 2007 the actual default rate rises from 0.59% to 1.56%, 3.2%, 5.9% and 13.2% across the five groups of predicted risk, so the ranking is still useful. But every group has moved up: the lowest fifth is about 12 times its rate in the calm years (0.05%) and the highest fifth about 5 times (2.77%). Stress hit even loans that looked safe.\n- In 2007 the reliability part of the Brier score (0.0029 for logistic) is larger than the resolution part (0.0023). So the cost of the wrong probabilities was bigger than the benefit of telling loans apart.\n\n**Does recalibration fix it?**\n- Platt scaling and isotonic regression fitted on the training years do almost nothing out of time. For the logistic model the ratio goes from 0.74, 0.42, 0.18 to 0.76, 0.43, 0.18 (Platt), and the 2007 log loss from 0.2235 to 0.2221. The reason is that in the calm years the model was already calibrated: the fitted Platt line is almost the identity (intercept -0.07, slope 0.98), so there is nothing to learn. Isotonic regression even makes the 2007 slope worse (0.63 against 0.71 for logistic).\n- A delayed base-rate correction does not help either. Using the default rate of the loan year two years earlier (the latest year whose outcomes would be complete) gives ratios of 0.41, 0.41 and 0.21. It is worse than nothing in 2005, no better in 2006 and slightly better in 2007, because defaults rose every year and an old rate is always too low.\n- An oracle that knew the true default rate would repair the average (ratio 1.00 by construction) and cut the 2007 log loss from 0.2235 to 0.1759, a fall of 21%. A straight-line recalibration fitted on the test year itself reaches 0.1729. So the level shift alone gets roughly 87% to 94% of what any straight-line recalibration could (94% for logistic and 87% for boosting in 2007). The slope problem is real but costs much less. After the level correction, the 2007 ECE is still 1.4 percentage points for logistic.\n- But the oracle needs the thing you are trying to forecast, the default rate of the new loans.\n\n**What this means for the project**\n- Calibration on past data does not guarantee calibration under shift. That is the pattern seen in the general machine learning literature, here in credit data.\n- The failure is mostly a change in the overall default rate that nothing in the borrower's features signals. A model trained on the calm years has no way to learn it, and fitting a better recalibration to the calm years cannot teach it either.\n- What would be needed is information about conditions: macroeconomic inputs, or monitoring that detects the change early (for example the default experience of recent loans) and a way to update quickly.\n\n**Cautions**\n- One crash in one dataset. The 24-month window of a 2006 or 2007 loan lies inside the downturn, and loans of one year share the same economy, so the intervals (especially the slope intervals) are too narrow.\n- The oracle versions use the test year's own outcomes. They show an upper limit, not a method.\n- A bank might have earlier signals than a complete two-year outcome (for example early delinquency of the newest loans). I did not test that, so the delayed correction here is a rough, pessimistic stand-in.\n- Reliability and ECE are noisy for the lowest-risk groups, which have few defaults. The in-time hold-out has only 209 defaults.\n- The explanation that stress compressed the differences between loans (slope below 1) is my reading of the numbers, not something I tested.\n- Not covered: whether uncertainty estimates from Bayesian or ensemble models could warn about the shift. That is the part of the project title I have not touched yet."
